==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{term|níu|lang=non}} (whence also Danish and Norwegian {{term|ni|lang=da}}; Faroese {{term|níggju|lang=fo}} and Swedish {{term|nio|lang=sv}}) from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{recons|newun|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|is}} {{recons|h₁néwn̥|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognates with Gothic {{term|𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌽|lang=got}}; Old English {{term|nigon|niġon|lang=ang}} (English {{term|nine|lang=en}}); Old Frisian {{term|nigun|lang=ofs}} (West Frisian {{term|njoggen|lang=fy}}); Old High German {{term|niun|lang=goh}} (German {{term|neun|lang=de}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈniːjʏ]|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|iːjʏ|lang=is}}
*: Homophones: [[nýju]]

===Numeral===
'''níu''' 

# '''[[nine]]'''; the cardinal number after [[átta#Icelandic|átta]] and before [[tíu#Icelandic|tíu]].

====Related terms====
* (''ordinal form'') [[níundi]] {{m}}, [[níunda]] {{f|n}}

====Derived terms====
* [[klukkan níu]]

